  "question": {
    "text": "A metal conductor of length $1 \\mathrm{~m}$ rotates vertically about one of its ends at angular velocity 5 radians per second. If the horizontal component of earth's magnetic field is $0.3 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~T}$, then the e.m.f. developed between the two ends of the conductor is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "FCp8H",
      "text": "\ndepends on the nature of the metal used\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "zPa2A",
      "text": "\ndepends on the intensity of the radiation\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "W4jeJ",
      "text": "\ndepends both on the intensity of the radiation and the metal used\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "STSYW",
      "text": "\nis the same for all metals and independent of the intensity of the radiation.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "emf. developed is given by\n$$\n\\varepsilon_{\\text {ind }}=\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{~B} \\omega \\mathrm{R}^2=50 \\mu \\mathrm{V} \\text {. }\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
